**Abstract** \({}^{\bullet}\) Funding agencies in North America and Europe are now committed to the promotion of a culture of sharing of qualitative interview data.This shifting perspective has proven to be controversial in some disciplines, especially where group authority and identity rest on fieldwork rather than on the secondary reuse or analysis of "other people's data" This article focuses primarily on the interpretative and creative value of online oral history databases or living archives, as well as their public or political value. In the twenty-first century, oral history archives need to be sites of data circulation and digital curation as well as living repositories dedicated to long-term preservation.

